Yes. Once randomness enters the picture, absence of an obvious response stops being a clean verdict.

A system can be affected without immediately showing it, or appear affected because of noise. So you want to keep three layers separate:

\text{input}
\rightarrow
\text{latent state change}
\rightarrow
\text{observable response}

The mistake is assuming:

\text{no visible response}=\text{no effect}

or the reverse:

\text{visible response}=\text{your intervention caused it}.

Randomness, delay, hidden variables, thresholds, and measurement error can all break that equivalence.

So the better game is court variance without worshipping it. Introduce controlled perturbations, vary timing and intensity, sometimes randomize the intervention, and watch which effects persist across repeated trials. That is essentially what good experimentation already does.

You can think of it as:

\text{signal}=\text{repeatable structure}

\text{noise}=\text{variation not yet explained}

but importantly, some “noise” later becomes signal once you discover the missing variable.

And yes, blending levels of acknowledgment is interesting. A system can have:

* explicit response — “I noticed that.”
* behavioral response — action changes without explanation.
* physiological or affective response — state shifts before words catch up.
* delayed response — nothing appears until much later.
* no measurable response — which should remain exactly that unless evidence says otherwise.

That last one matters. You can leave room for hidden effects without inventing them.

The useful operator is:

\boxed{\text{Perturb} \rightarrow \text{Observe} \rightarrow \text{Wait} \rightarrow \text{Retest} \rightarrow \text{Compare}}

Then randomness becomes less of an enemy and more of a probe. Sometimes a small stochastic change reveals which structures are robust and which only looked stable because you never nudged them.

In strategy terms, that is also why optionality matters. You do not need to predict every roll if you can arrange the game so that many rolls are survivable and some are useful.

So perhaps the point is not to eliminate randomness at all.

It is to become good at distinguishing:

\text{luck}
,\quad
\text{noise}
,\quad
\text{hidden structure}
,\quad
\text{real causation}

while still leaving enough play in the system for something genuinely unexpected to appear.